From: roc-native-macos engine patch series
Subject: [PATCH 0004] Write a table in place
Upstream: not yet sent (proposed in https://github.com/lukewilliamboswell/roc-signals/issues/308)
Applies to: roc-signals a13ff70e + 0001 + 0002 + 0003, platform/TableCore.roc,
  platform/Engine.roc, platform/Core.roc

What
  Three places that take a table apart, changed so the table's rows reach
  the write uniquely held. Nothing else changes: same commands, same
  turns, same changes recorded.
  - `TableCore.write` takes the table apart with `match` instead of a
    destructuring statement, and records the key in the change record
    with `contains` and `insert` instead of `Dict.update`.
  - `Engine.inner` (the public table to its representation) is a `match`
    instead of a parameter pattern.
  - The table cell's lens (`Core.table_of`) keeps the table it began
    unwrapped and wraps it only to call the modifier, instead of wrapping
    it, unwrapping a second copy for `TableCore.mark` and passing the
    wrapped one on.

Why
  A turn that wrote k rows of a table cost the size of the table: every
  `Table.update` copied the table's rows (a 40,000,000-byte `memcpy` at
  a million rows in bench/rows, lldb on a --debug build), and the first
  write of each turn copied them once more. Three compiler behaviours,
  each in optimized builds:
  - A record taken apart by a destructuring statement or a parameter
    pattern keeps its refcounted fields shared until the function
    returns, when the record has two or more of them. `write`'s
    `{ rows, record, .. } = table` left `rows` shared for the insert, so
    a write in a loop (an app's `var $t` loop of `Table.update`, as
    bench/rows ticks) copied the rows on every write. A `match` moves the
    fields out. Reproducer: patches/roc-compiler, destructure-sharing.
  - `Engine.inner`'s parameter pattern `|{ t }| t` is the same: inlined
    into the lens, it left the representation shared until the lens
    returned, and the first write of the turn copied it.
  - The lens passed the modifier a table it still held: `began` was
    wrapped, read through a second unwrap for `mark`, and the wrapped
    value increfed for the call (lldb: an incref of the rows just before
    the modifier's call, and the copy in the modifier's first insert).
  The change record's `Dict.update` copies a record it shares when the
  record grows (a compiler issue of its own: patches/roc-compiler,
  dict-update-sharing; folded in here because it is the same lines): it
  costs a turn that writes many keys, not the turn of a few writes
  measured below, and it made building a table with `Table.insert` in a
  loop quadratic (bench/rows `-rows-build insert`, mount engine time:
  0.3 s to 19 ms at 10,000 rows, 53 s to 0.15 s at 100,000).

Measured (engine ms p50 of a turn that writes 3 rows with `Table.update`,
reproducer app: a table cell of N rows, a button whose handler updates 3
rows; `none` draws nothing of the table, `reader` reads one row through
`cells.rows.signal().map(...)`, `view` draws every row with a
`table_view`; before = 0001 + 0002 + 0003)
                    N=10,000   N=100,000   N=1,000,000
  none   before       0.3         3.3         31.1
  none   after        0.0         0.0          0.0
  reader before       0.3         3.6         35.2
  reader after        0.0         0.0          0.0
  view   before       0.8        12.2        112.7
  view   after        0.3         2.9         44.6   (patch 0005: 0.1)
  With only the change record's `contains` + `insert` (no `match`s, no
  lens change), `none` is 7.6 and 46.7 ms at 100,000 and a million.
  A `table_view` still costs the table's size after this patch; patch
  0005 removes that.

---
diff --git a/platform/Core.roc b/platform/Core.roc
index fa24b8b..370763c 100644
--- a/platform/Core.roc
+++ b/platform/Core.roc
@@ -429,9 +429,11 @@ Core := [].{
 				modify: |w, f| modify(
 					w,
 					|t| {
-						began = wrap(TableCore.begin_changes(unwrap(t)))
-						before = TableCore.mark(unwrap(began))
-						wrap(TableCore.kept(unwrap(f(began)), before))
+						# `began` stays unwrapped until the call, so the modifier
+						# receives the table's only reference and writes in place.
+						began = TableCore.begin_changes(unwrap(t))
+						before = TableCore.mark(began)
+						wrap(TableCore.kept(unwrap(f(wrap(began))), before))
 					},
 				),
 			},
diff --git a/platform/Engine.roc b/platform/Engine.roc
index bc8c15e..ba85b4a 100644
--- a/platform/Engine.roc
+++ b/platform/Engine.roc
@@ -1828,8 +1828,12 @@ wrap_cell : Core.Cell(st, a) -> Engine.Cell(st, a)
 wrap_cell = |c| { c: c }
 
 ## Move the public table's representation into the engine; no rows are copied.
+## A `match`, not a parameter pattern: inlined, a pattern leaves the
+## representation shared until the caller returns.
 inner : Engine.Table(k, v) -> TableCore.Table(k, v)
-inner = |{ t }| t
+inner = |table| match table {
+	{ t } => t
+}
 
 ## Wrap the uniquely held representation without retaining another reference.
 outer : TableCore.Table(k, v) -> Engine.Table(k, v)
diff --git a/platform/TableCore.roc b/platform/TableCore.roc
index e429e88..5ec686c 100644
--- a/platform/TableCore.roc
+++ b/platform/TableCore.roc
@@ -227,26 +227,24 @@ TableCore := [].{
 
 ## Remember `key`'s state when the turn began (its first write wins) and
 ## apply the write: `Ok(row)` stores the row, `Err(Missing)` removes it.
+## The table is taken apart by `match`: a destructuring statement leaves
+## its dictionaries shared until the function returns, so the insert
+## would copy every row. The change record takes the key with `contains`
+## and `insert`, not `Dict.update`, which copies a record it shares.
 write : Table(k, v), k, [Absent, Was(v)], Try(v, [Missing]) -> Table(k, v)
 	where [k.is_eq : k, k -> Bool, k.to_hash : k, Hasher -> Hasher]
-write = |table, key, before, after| {
-	{ rows, record, whole, version: now, start, id, phase } = table
-	next_record = match whole {
-		Rewritten => record
-		Keyed =>
-			record.update(
-				key,
-				|found| match found {
-					Ok(first) => Ok(first)
-					Err(Missing) => Ok(before)
-				},
-			)
-	}
-	next_rows = match after {
-		Ok(row) => rows.insert(key, row)
-		Err(Missing) => rows.remove(key)
+write = |table, key, before, after| match table {
+	{ rows, record, whole, version: now, start, id, phase } => {
+		next_record = match whole {
+			Rewritten => record
+			Keyed => if (record.contains(key)) record else record.insert(key, before)
+		}
+		next_rows = match after {
+			Ok(row) => rows.insert(key, row)
+			Err(Missing) => rows.remove(key)
+		}
+		{ rows: next_rows, record: next_record, whole, version: now + 1, start, id, phase }
 	}
-	{ rows: next_rows, record: next_record, whole, version: now + 1, start, id, phase }
 }
 
 ## How many keys a table's changes hold, or `Rewritten`.
